meta {
  name: A name another member holds
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

put {
  url: {{groupService}}/members
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "groupKey": "{{groupKey}}",
    "memberSecret": "{{secondMemberSecret}}",
    "displayName": "{{displayName}}"
  }
}

script:pre-request {
  const CryptoJS = require("crypto-js");
  if (!bru.getEnvVar("secondMemberSecret")) {
    bru.setEnvVar("secondMemberSecret", CryptoJS.lib.WordArray.random(32).toString(CryptoJS.enc.Base64));
  }
}

tests {
  test("refuses the name and stores nothing", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(409);
    expect(res.body.error).to.equal("that name is taken in this group");
  });
}

docs {
  # A name another member holds

  A second member claiming the name the first holds, which is refused.

  Identity cannot be forged inside a group: this request derives its own member id from its own secret, so what it could take is the label other members read and never the membership behind it.
  First claim wins, the refusal is 409 rather than 400 because the request is well formed, and nothing is stored.

  An app answers it by asking its user for another name.

  A name whose holder's lease lapsed is a name nobody holds, so the same request succeeds once the first member stops refreshing.
}
